176 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (Several researchers have proposed IoT-based solutions for improving waste management. BIN-CT \(2019\) focused on predicting the fill level of waste containers using data and machine-learning techniques mainly to improve waste collection and route planning. However the work is primarily focused on prediction and city-level collection optimization.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
177 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (An Based Smart Waste Management System Using LoRa and TensorFlow \(2020\) introduced sensors, communication technology and deep learning for monitoring and classifying waste but the system requires several components and has a relatively complex architecture. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
178 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (Smart Waste Management Scheme using IoT for Metropolitan Cities \(2023\) proposed IoT-enabled garbage bins for monitoring waste levels and improving collection efficiency. Mainly addressed large-scale metropolitan waste management rather than a simple low-cost prototype)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
179 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E ( A Smart Bin with Real-Time Monitoring and Garbage Level Tracking using IoT \(2024\) used weight sensors to monitor the amount of waste in real time. While it provides monitoring its main focus is on the current status of the bin rather than predicting when it will become full.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 61 0 R
/E (Recently Predictive Analysis-Based Sustainable Waste Management using IoT Edge Computing and Blockchain \(2025\) combined IoT, edge computing and machine-learning techniques for waste prediction and collection scheduling but its architecture is comparatively complex for a small-scale implementation.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 62 0 R
/T (Based on these studies there is an opportunity to develop an low-cost predictive smart-bin system that combines multiple sensor parameters with local monitoring and fullness prediction. Our proposed system addresses this by using an ESP32 with fill-level, weight, temperature, humidity and air-quality sensors along with an OLED, LEDs and buzzer for status indication. This one also aims to keep the design affordable and scalable for communities or pilot projects. It uses processing to reduce dependency on cloud services and allows faster response times through real-time alerts. )
/E (Based on these studies there is an opportunity to develop an low-cost predictive smart-bin system that combines multiple sensor parameters with local monitoring and fullness prediction. Our proposed system addresses this by using an ESP32 with fill-level, weight, temperature, humidity and air-quality sensors along with an OLED, LEDs and buzzer for status indication. This one also aims to keep the design affordable and scalable for communities or pilot projects. It uses processing to reduce dependency on cloud services and allows faster response times through real-time alerts. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 62 0 R
/T (The system not tracks the current fill level but also predicts when the bin will reach capacity based on usage patterns. This helps optimize collection schedules without requiring human oversight. It supports detection of issues like high temperature or poor air quality inside the bin, which could indicate fire risks or biological decomposition problems. By integrating sensors and using lightweight machine-learning models it delivers accurate predictions while staying cost-effective. The goal is to create a solution that works well in both urban and rural areas where waste management resources are limited. This system can be easily installed in spaces, residential buildings or small commercial zones. Its modular design allows upgrades, such, as adding solar power or connectivity options. Overall this approach brings together simplicity, affordability and intelligence to make waste management smarter and more sustainable.)
/E (The system not tracks the current fill level but also predicts when the bin will reach capacity based on usage patterns. This helps optimize collection schedules without requiring human oversight. It supports detection of issues like high temperature or poor air quality inside the bin, which could indicate fire risks or biological decomposition problems. By integrating sensors and using lightweight machine-learning models it delivers accurate predictions while staying cost-effective. The goal is to create a solution that works well in both urban and rural areas where waste management resources are limited. This system can be easily installed in spaces, residential buildings or small commercial zones. Its modular design allows upgrades, such, as adding solar power or connectivity options. Overall this approach brings together simplicity, affordability and intelligence to make waste management smarter and more sustainable.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
